meta {
  name: Search Products - Filters
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

get {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products?_fields=id&_fields=sku&sku=BAG-10157
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.data[0].sku: eq BAG-10157
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-07 against "David's Dev Account".
  
  ## What it does
  `GET /api/v3/products?sku=BAG-10157` -- a plain field-equality filter.
  Confirmed working: returns exactly the one matching product.
  
  ## Filter dialect findings (Phase 0)
  `API V3.md`'s filter table (~lines 145-186) lists a "Current" (JSON
  object) and "Possible Alternative" (query-string) syntax for each filter
  kind. v3 implements the **query-string "Possible Alternative" dialect**;
  the JSON-object "Current" syntax was not tested against this endpoint
  (GET has no body). Confirmed live:
  - **Plain field filter**: `sku=BAG-10157` -- 200, exact match (this
    request).
  - **Operator filter**: `created[gt]=2026-01-01` -- 200, correctly
    filters by date (URL-encode the brackets, e.g. via curl's
    `--data-urlencode`).
  - **Related-entity dot filter**: `categories.name=Backpacks` -- 200,
    matches products in that category. Confirms the
    `related-entity.attribute[filter]=value` alternative from the "Related
    entities filtering transversal" section (~line 148).
  - **Custom attribute filter**: `attributes.color=Black` (plain) and
    `attributes.brand[icontains]=Plytix` (operator) -- both 200 and
    correctly filter by the named custom attribute's value. Confirms the
    `attributes.name[<filter>]=value`-shaped dialect (~line 150), except
    the dot-segment names the actual attribute (e.g. `color`, `brand`),
    not the literal string `name`.
  
  ## `_fields`/`_expand` edge cases found
  - `_fields=category_ids` (declared in `openapi_pimv3.json`'s
    `ProductOutputDto`) is **rejected**: `400
    {"errors":[{"name":"HiddenFieldError","description":"Field
    'category_ids' does not exist in model 'Product'."}]}`. The plural
    `_fields=categories` works and returns fully expanded category
    objects instead.
  - `_fields=relationships` (the spec's declared field name) is **silently
    dropped** -- 200, no error, but the field just doesn't appear in the
    response. The real field is `_fields=product_relationships` (see
    `Get Product by ID.bru`). Two different "wrong field" behaviors on
    this one endpoint: some invalid `_fields` values 400 with a named
    error, others are silently ignored.
  - `_expand=<relationship>` (the mechanism `API V3.md` describes for
    default-expand behavior) is **rejected on this endpoint**: `400
    {"errors":[{"name":"InvalidFieldError","description":"Field '_expand'
    does not exist in model 'Product'."}]}`. Expansion instead appears
    automatic per-`_fields` value (requesting `_fields=categories` already
    returns fully expanded objects with no `_expand` needed).
  
  See `config/api-testing.config.md` quirks #10-13 for the full log.
}
